;; Who may commit to this channel.
;;
;; `guix pull' and `guix time-machine' authenticate every commit they fetch
;; against this file AS IT STOOD IN THE PARENT COMMIT -- so a key can only ever
;; be added by somebody this file already authorises, and an attacker who can
;; write to the repository still cannot make a commit that guix will accept.
;;
;; THE FINGERPRINT IS THE SIGNING SUBKEY'S, not the primary's, and that is the
;; opposite of what it looks like it should be. guix does NOT resolve a
;; signature back to the certifying primary key: `authenticate-commit' compares
;; (openpgp-public-key-fingerprint (commit-signing-key ...)) -- the fingerprint
;; of whatever key actually made the signature -- against this list. The
;; introduction in the consumer's channels.scm is checked by the same function
;; and therefore takes the same fingerprint.
;;
;; It could not be the primary here in any case: that key is certify-only and
;; cannot sign, so gpg always reaches for this subkey.
;;
;; CONSEQUENCE FOR ROTATION: replacing the signing subkey means editing this
;; file, in a commit signed by the OUTGOING subkey, before the new one can sign
;; anything. The primary's fingerprint never appears here, so renewing or
;; re-certifying the primary does not touch it.
;;
;; Verified the hard way: an earlier version of this file named the primary,
;; every commit was properly signed, the file parsed, and `guix time-machine'
;; refused the channel with
;;   initial commit ... is signed by 'DA12 ...' instead of '7652 ...'
;;
;; The matching public key lives on the `keyring' branch of this repository,
;; which is where guix looks for it -- adding a fingerprint here without adding
;; the key there authorises somebody guix cannot identify.
;;
;; A consumer only trusts any of this because of the channel INTRODUCTION, which
;; pins a fingerprint and a commit out of band. See README.md.

(authorizations
 (version 0)

 (("DA12 410C ACFB 0E1D 46C0  944A 9CE0 4DC9 D65C A7EF"
   (name "ryancraig"))))
